      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a revised version of the critical comments:\n\nThe proposed LongRecall architecture relies on a misguided optimization of the linear attention mechanism, which appears to come at the cost of significant information loss in the global memory bottleneck. By compressing the memory token, the authors implicitly adopt a чercul(izable discretization scheme, which likely introduces severe artifacts in the retrieved representations, particularly at the boundaries of the sliding window. Furthermore, the global memory's reduced capacity imposes strict limitations on the length of the input sequence, rendering LongRecall incompatible with many potential use cases.\n\nA more detailed examination of the sliding window approach reveals that it may not be the optimal solution for efficient attention computation, especially in scenarios where the sequence length far exceeds the window size. The authors' decision to keep the window size fixed and to reuse the output from previous positions in the sequence may lead to incorrect information alignment, thereby compromising the overall retrieval accuracy. Additionally, the omission of any albeit comparison to standard attention mechanisms, such as multi-head attention or parallel attention, raises questions about the scalability of LongRecall in the long run.\n\nMoreover, the authors' claim that the proposed O(n) complexity is achieved at the cost of O(k) memory requirements, where k is the number",
